+R34 UFS CPU / governor decision — 2026-11-20
+
+Current decision: keep the working `bootcpu=1 cpus=1` configuration while main identifies the work consuming its CPU. Main reports integer-loop **2473 µs**, memcpy **3499 MB/s**, matching R15, and approximately **99% CPU busy**. These are main's supplied findings; this investigation did not reread or benchmark the current USB stream. They argue against a new fast-core throughput collapse, but do not identify the busy thread, prove a particular MHz, or exclude intermittent throttling.
+
+**The current UFS architecture supports at most three XNU cores.** Four physical cores exist, but the resident loader on affinity `0x0` services the UEFI storage mailbox while XNU runs on another core. `cpus=4` does not remove that service dependency.
+
+| Decision | Exact local source |
+|---|---|
+| Four physical affinities are `0x0, 0x1, 0x100, 0x101`; `bootcpu=1` selects affinity `0x1`, not XNU logical CPU 1. | [cpu-map.inc:2](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/actual/devices/lithium/diagnostics/cpu-map.inc:2), [patched loader.c:3256](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/actual/xnuloader/loader.c:3256) |
+| The patched loader rejects UFS boot on its resident core, skips that core when enumerating secondaries, and accepts only PSCI-reported OFF candidates. | [loader.c:3623](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/actual/xnuloader/loader.c:3623), lines 3626–3653 |
+| The resident core polls the mailbox and invokes UEFI ReadBlocks / WriteBlocks / FlushBlocks. Its watch loop also handles watchdog, optional charging, and USB work. | [loader.c:2328](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/actual/xnuloader/loader.c:2328), lines 2330–2424 and 2597–2639 |
+| XNU's bridge has one shared request at a time; it actively waits up to 2 ms, then sleeps in 1 ms intervals. | [hakios.ufs.c:25](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/actual/drivers/storage/hakios.ufs.c:25), [hakios.ufs.c:395](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/actual/drivers/storage/hakios.ufs.c:395) |
+
+With `bootcpu=1`, the maximum candidate order is `[0x1, 0x100, 0x101]`, conditional on PSCI eligibility and successful XNU startup. R14 already demonstrated three running XNU CPUs, but its two `0x100/0x101` cores took approximately **66.1×** as long for the integer loop, with MIDR `0x512f2051` versus `0x512f2011` on `0x1`. See [R14 diagnosis:31](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/local/reports/hakios-ios16-r14-final-diagnosis.md:31), CPU mapping and benchmark table immediately below. This is historical evidence, not a fresh measurement of those cores. Enabling three cores is a small configuration change with an unresolved performance outcome; it is not a demonstrated latency fix.
+
+Exposing all four to XNU requires replacing the resident UFS service and transferring its other necessary duties, with controller/DMA/cache/interrupt ownership and return handling accounted for. A native XNU UFS path is a possible architecture, but this is substantial storage/handoff work, not a scheduler or governor switch. The reserved core is a property of this implementation, not an intrinsic MSM8996 hardware limit.
+
+**The existing power driver cannot provide the requested MSM8996 governor.** [hakios.cpufreq.c:1](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/actual/drivers/power/hakios.cpufreq.c:1) explicitly targets redwood EPSS; lines 57–63 and 95–107 use its domain registers and write the top frequency-table row. Its domain mapping at lines 80–86 also does not describe lithium's two-cluster affinity layout. This is a fixed boost operation, not an adaptive MSM8996 policy.
+
+The exact vendor pin is MiCode `1492c8e4373b7428bb0accc4f4881e97b36508b9`. The required path is:
+
+| Layer | Source facts and location |
+|---|---|
+| Policy → clock provider | [qcom-cpufreq.c:48](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/vendor/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq.c:48): target → round rate → `clk_set_rate`; lines 140–148 group CPUs sharing a clock. |
+| Lithium DT / CPU mux / APCC PLL | [stock DTS:5281](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/local/artifacts/stock/V9.5.2.0.NAHCNFA/lithium-stock.dts:5281): separate CPU tables, `qcom,cpu-clock-8996-pro`, named PLL/mux resources and supplies. [clock-cpu-8996.c:436](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/vendor/drivers/clk/msm/clock-cpu-8996.c:436): LF/HF mux parents; lines 789–820 and 894–921 map `0/1` to pwrcl and `0x100/101` to perfcl. Those names do not measure current speed. |
+| Voltage / OPP / silicon bin | [clock-cpu-8996.c:1142](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/vendor/drivers/clk/msm/clock-cpu-8996.c:1142): regulator dependencies; lines 1198–1227 create OPPs from frequency/corner/voltage; lines 1327–1371 select efuse-dependent plans. [clock.c:681](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/vendor/drivers/clk/msm/clock.c:681) enforces target voltage requirements around rate changes. CPR/Kryo retention and mem-acc dependencies are in [cpr3-hmss-regulator.c:1226](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/vendor/drivers/regulator/cpr3-hmss-regulator.c:1226), lines 1226–1365. |
+| Secure access and transitions | [clock-cpu-8996.c:319](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/vendor/drivers/clk/msm/clock-cpu-8996.c:319): SCM versus direct access depends on the mux configuration; [scm.c:1081](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/vendor/drivers/soc/qcom/scm.c:1081) implements secure I/O. Pro selects the v3/pro branch at lines 1535–1538; do not assume every access uses SCM. Lines 675–743 implement ordered parent/PLL changes; lines 703–711 explicitly explain an undervoltage hazard from incorrect transition order. |
+
+**Minimal next step:** main should use its existing current-run observations to identify the dominant process/thread and separate runnable CPU work from UFS bridge waiting. High CPU utilization can include mailbox spinning; the bridge's active-wait source above is a concrete candidate, not an attribution. Do not duplicate main's measurements.
+
+For the next separately authorized diagnostic revision, add a bounded per-core snapshot of MPIDR/MIDR, **SCTLR_EL1 cache/MMU enables**, MAIR/TCR and the same short workload; retain `0x1` as control and characterize `0x100/101` before using them for UI scheduling. Existing [cpu-sanity.inc:40](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/actual/devices/lithium/diagnostics/cpu-sanity.inc:40) already binds samples and reads MPIDR/CNTFRQ/MAIR/TCR, but does not read SCTLR. This yields useful cache-versus-throughput evidence before a governor port.
+
+If clock state is then needed, first specify a small read-only snapshot of the stock-DT-named mux/PLL mode/lock/L/alpha/divider and efuse fields using the matched pro layout. Treat those as configuration evidence, not independently measured MHz; MMIO access permission remains to be established. Do not call vendor initialization, clock/debug-mux enable, SCM writes, or regulator operations as “measurement”: they change hardware. Debug measurement enable itself writes registers ([clock-cpu-8996.c:389](F:/IOS-MIX1/evidence/touch-live-20261120/latency-cpu/vendor/drivers/clk/msm/clock-cpu-8996.c:389)).
+
+All writes from this investigation are confined to this directory: note, source snapshots, and manifests. Vendor copies were checked against Git blob IDs at the pinned commit. The `actual/` sources are snapshots of the patched WSL workspace, distinct from the unpatched upstream checkout; they are not a new binary-to-source attestation. No production edits, build, device query, USB action, or current-run benchmark was performed.
